WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.033 --> 00:00:05.373
Remove one wing bud from a chick embryo.

2
00:00:06.353 --> 00:00:08.353
The neurons serving that limb come out fewer.

3
00:00:09.333 --> 00:00:10.333
Were they never made?

4
00:00:11.013 --> 00:00:12.493
Or were they made, and then lost?

5
00:00:13.167 --> 00:00:19.567
First, two words. A target is the tissue an axon grows to and connects with, such as

6
00:00:19.567 --> 00:00:20.807
a limb muscle or a gland.

7
00:00:21.500 --> 00:00:26.420
A ganglion is a cluster of neuron cell bodies outside the brain and spinal cord.

8
00:00:27.033 --> 00:00:30.993
Spinal ganglia sit beside the cord and hold sensory neurons.

9
00:00:31.953 --> 00:00:35.693
Sympathetic ganglia hold neurons of the sympathetic nervous system.

10
00:00:36.267 --> 00:00:42.147
In 1934, Viktor Hamburger removed one wing bud from chick embryos about three days

11
00:00:42.147 --> 00:00:43.050
old.

12
00:00:43.050 --> 00:00:46.970
On the operated side, the neurons serving that limb came out reduced.

13
00:00:48.650 --> 00:00:53.530
Hamburger first read this as the limb controlling how many cells grew in the spinal

14
00:00:53.530 --> 00:00:55.050
cord and its ganglia.

15
00:00:55.683 --> 00:00:57.743
But one count at the end hides a choice.

16
00:00:58.543 --> 00:01:00.763
A small ganglion has two possible histories.

17
00:01:01.517 --> 00:01:04.197
Predict. What are the two histories?

18
00:01:05.157 --> 00:01:06.817
And what would you measure to tell them apart?

19
00:01:12.467 --> 00:01:14.447
Fewer neurons might have been made.

20
00:01:14.447 --> 00:01:17.807
Or the normal number might have been made, and then lost.

21
00:01:18.417 --> 00:01:20.577
Both give a small ganglion at the end.

22
00:01:21.337 --> 00:01:23.817
To separate them, you count at a series of stages.

23
00:01:24.550 --> 00:01:28.250
You also mark the cells that are dividing, and the cells that are dying.

24
00:01:28.883 --> 00:01:34.943
In 1949, Hamburger and Rita Levi-Montalcini did exactly that, in chick spinal

25
00:01:34.943 --> 00:01:35.943
ganglia.

26
00:01:36.017 --> 00:01:40.637
They followed the ganglia stage by stage, after removing or adding limb tissue.

27
00:01:41.250 --> 00:01:44.990
Many neurons formed and grew fibres before the deficit appeared.

28
00:01:45.683 --> 00:01:48.563
Degenerating cells became conspicuous later.

29
00:01:49.167 --> 00:01:50.667
The neurons had been made.

30
00:01:51.587 --> 00:01:52.587
Then they were lost.

31
00:01:55.950 --> 00:01:58.110
So the limb seemed to keep neurons alive.

32
00:01:59.010 --> 00:02:01.030
It was not only calling them into existence.

33
00:02:01.700 --> 00:02:03.140
Three conclusions follow.

34
00:02:04.280 --> 00:02:06.400
First, neurons are made in excess, and then lost.

35
00:02:07.033 --> 00:02:09.533
Second, whether they are kept depends on the target.

36
00:02:10.133 --> 00:02:13.053
Third, the target must supply something neurons need.

37
00:02:13.913 --> 00:02:17.373
If it supplies too little for all of them, they must in effect compete.

38
00:02:18.017 --> 00:02:21.137
Neither the something nor the competition had been shown.

39
00:02:21.137 --> 00:02:22.777
They were inferences.

40
00:02:23.517 --> 00:02:27.717
The neurotrophic hypothesis would have to earn them, one test at a time.

41
00:02:28.267 --> 00:02:30.407
Here is the one idea to carry through.

42
00:02:30.407 --> 00:02:34.027
Make too many, then let the target keep what it can feed.

43
00:02:34.650 --> 00:02:37.450
The test case is nerve growth factor, NGF.

44
00:02:38.670 --> 00:02:42.490
Acting as a survival factor, it decides how many neurons made stay alive.

45
00:02:43.150 --> 00:02:46.170
It can never raise that number above the number made.

46
00:02:46.733 --> 00:02:49.633
So a small ganglion is a question about history.

47
00:02:50.593 --> 00:02:52.013
Count across time before you decide.
